+"""Pattern-graph matcher for compound (multi-call) requests.
+
+Architectural role (paper §4.2 "How compound requests are handled"):
+
+    A compound request is not a single LLM call but a graph of LLM and
+    tool invocations linked by data dependencies, of which only a prefix
+    has executed at the moment a scheduling decision must be made.
+    Per-call length prediction is therefore not the binding constraint;
+    what matters is whether the sum of costs across all remaining stages
+    will fit within the program-level deadline.
+
+    JITServe addresses this by maintaining a database of historical
+    (graph pattern, observed runtime) pairs, indexed for fast retrieval
+    via locality-sensitive hashing in DynamicClustering (see
+    similarity.py). At every scheduling tick, the partial execution graph
+    of each in-progress compound request is matched against the
+    historical record. The match returns two values:
+
+        (1) the predicted cost of the immediately next stage, which
+            informs L_up_hat for the current call; and
+
+        (2) the predicted aggregate cost of all the stages that remain,
+            which is subtracted from the program-level deadline to
+            produce the time budget actually available to the current
+            stage -- i.e., the delta_t that ultimately enters b_min in
+            the scheduling formula.
+
+    Without this aggregate prediction, a per-call length bound would
+    tell the scheduler nothing about whether the program as a whole will
+    meet its deadline.
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+    - GraphMatchContext (class below): owns the historical (graph, runtime)
+      database (collection_graph_set) and the unfinished-graph trackers
+      for both ToT-style and DeepResearch-style compound workloads.
+
+    - calculate_stage_ratio() (below): the entry point that GMAX calls
+      every tick. Returns the fraction of program budget that should be
+      attributed to the current stage. Internally branches on
+      graph_matching_mode in {"none", "precise", "static", "online"}:
+      static and online both invoke dynamic_clustering.find_best_match
+      to identify the closest historical (graph, runtime) record.
+
+    - track_collection_completion() (below): incrementally extends the
+      historical (graph, runtime) database as compound programs finish.
+      A finished structure is converted to a Graph, added to
+      collection_graph_set, and registered with the LSH-style
+      DynamicClustering retriever.
+"""
+
 from __future__ import annotations
 
 from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Set, Tuple
