+"""Quantile Regression Forest (QRF) backend for the response-length upper bound.
+
+Architectural role (paper §4.2 "How L_up is estimated"):
+
+    Rather than predicting the expected response length and treating that
+    estimate as exact, JITServe asks a Quantile Regression Forest to provide
+    a conservative upper bound L_up_hat -- typically the P95 of the
+    conditional length distribution, given the prompt features (prompt token
+    count, model identity, and lexical signatures derived from the prompt).
+    The QRF executes in single-digit milliseconds and is therefore
+    inexpensive enough to remain on the request's critical path; an
+    8B-parameter LLM used as a length oracle would itself be slower than
+    starting to produce the response. The bound is refined at each
+    subsequent scheduling tick as additional decoded tokens reveal the
+    trajectory of the actual response, which causes the P5-P95 envelope to
+    contract toward the ground truth.
+
+    The asymmetry of this design is deliberate: an underestimate of length
+    leads to insufficient reserved bandwidth and an irrecoverable SLO
+    violation, whereas an overestimate produces only a brief over-reservation,
+    the surplus from which is returned to the capacity pool on the next tick.
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+    - predict() (below): the actual QRF call; returns the P95 quantile of
+      the conditional output-length distribution given the input prompt.
+      The model.predict(..., quantiles=[0.95]) call is the literal source
+      of L_up_hat referenced throughout the rest of the system.
+
+    - async_predict() (below): asynchronous wrapper that updates
+      RequestInfo.output_len after the QRF returns, so the scheduler can
+      consume the upper bound on the next tick without blocking.
+
+    - The QRF model and vectorizer are loaded once at startup
+      (load_model()) and kept resident; the assets/qrf/ directory in the
+      repo holds pre-trained QRF models per (dataset, target LLM) pair.
+"""
+
