+"""Scheduling-policy implementations -- the GMAX scheduler and its baselines.
+
+This module contains both JITServe's own scheduling rule (SLOPolicy below,
+named "concord" / "jitserve" / "slo" in the policy registry) and the
+single-objective baselines compared against in §3.3 of the paper:
+
+    - FCFSPolicy   -- vLLM-style first-come-first-served (paper §3.3 row 1)
+    - SJFPolicy    -- shortest-job-first using QRF length prediction;
+                       referenced in the paper as "LTR" (Length-To-Run),
+                       the strongest non-JITServe baseline (§3.3 row 3)
+    - SRTFPolicy   -- shortest-remaining-time, deadline-driven variant
+    - LASPolicy    -- program-level Least Attained Service (Autellix's
+                       PLAS, paper §3.3 row 4)
+    - SLOPolicy    -- JITServe's GMAX scheduler
+
+Architectural role of SLOPolicy (paper §4.2 "The meaning of b_min" and §6
+"GMAX -- The Scheduling Algorithm"):
+
+    The minimum-serving-bandwidth formula given in the paper
+
+            b_min(r, t) = (L_up_hat(r, t) - L_done(r, t)) / delta_t(r, t)
+
+    is implemented here as a finish-time predictor combined with a
+    deadline-ratio penalty rather than as a literal bandwidth variable.
+    The mathematics is equivalent: for each request, SLOPolicy computes
+
+        remain_len  = predict_output_len - decode_len           # = L_up - L_done
+        remain_time = unit_time * remain_len                     # predicted wall-clock to finish at current rate
+        predict_finish = serve_time + remain_time                # when the request will actually complete
+        ratio       = deadline / predict_finish                  # >1 means comfortable, <1 means late
+
+    and then weights the request's reward (the marginal-goodput function
+    in §6.3) by ratio raised to penalty_factor. A request with shrinking
+    slack receives an exponentially larger priority, which is the
+    just-in-time semantics described in §4.2: b_min is recomputed every
+    tick because L_done grows and remain_time shrinks, so a request that
+    began with comfortable slack may be promoted to high priority later
+    if its actual response is approaching the QRF upper bound.
+
+    The same get_priority() handles all three SLO classes (LATENCY,
+    THROUGHPUT, COLLECTIVE in this codebase, corresponding to
+    latency-sensitive, deadline-sensitive, and compound in the paper).
+    The only per-class difference is in how delta_t is constructed --
+    handled by _compute_remain_time() below, which calls
+    allocation_control() for COLLECTIVE / compound requests so that the
+    delta_t available to the current stage already accounts for the
+    predicted aggregate cost of downstream stages, as supplied by
+    GraphMatchContext (see request_analyzer/graph_context.py).
+"""
+
